# Cursor AI Coding Rules - 90% Per-File Unit Test Coverage

- Whenever you modify, refactor, or add code to any file, ALWAYS add/update unit tests.
- Every single file must reach >= 90% unit test coverage across all 4 parameters:
  - Statements >= 90%
  - Branches >= 90%
  - Functions >= 90%
  - Lines >= 90%
- Never skip any file.
- The test configuration enforces `perFile: true` and will throw an error if coverage on any file is below 90%.
- A global timeout of 10000ms is enforced for all unit tests.
- After any code change, verify coverage by running:
  `npm run test:coverage`
- Do not stop until all tests pass and >= 90% per-file coverage is achieved.

# Centralized Structured Logging Rules
- No raw `console.log` / `console.error` calls.
- Use `logger` from `backend/src/utils/logger.ts` or `frontendLogger` from `frontend/src/utils/logger.ts`.
- Include structured context: `timestamp`, `level`, `service`, `message`, `requestId`, `context`, `durationMs`, and serialized `error` stack.
- Local persistence: All logs persist to the file system (`logs/app.log`, `logs/error.log`, `logs/app-YYYY-MM-DD.log`) when running locally.
- Compliance: Automatically purge logs older than the retention window (14 days default).
- Apply detailed logs across all controllers, services, database queries, and middleware.

# CI/CD Pipeline & Pull Request Rules
- CI/CD workflow `.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on pull requests to validate linting, typechecking, building, and 90% per-file unit test coverage.
- All CI/CD workflows and jobs must enforce an explicit timeout (`timeout-minutes: 15`).
- The workflow must post and update an executive summary PR comment with unit test pass/fail counts, execution duration, and overall code coverage.

# User Prompt History Rules
- Maintain `prompts.md` in the workspace root for saving user-provided prompts.
- Save ONLY user-provided prompts in `prompts.md`. Do NOT save AI-generated conversations, responses, or thoughts.
- Append each user prompt in chronological sequence with clean headers.

# Internationalization (i18n) & UI_STRINGS Rules
- Never hardcode user-facing literal strings in components/JSX.
- Move all literal strings to `frontend/src/constants/uiStrings.ts` accessible through `UI_STRINGS`.
- Use template placeholders/functions for dynamic runtime substitution.
- Update tests to assert against `UI_STRINGS` constants rather than literal strings.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all constants and helper modules.

# Separate Constants File Rules
- Keep all application constants, defaults, lookup tables, and palettes in separate files under `constants/`.
- Never inline magic numbers or configuration dictionaries directly in components, services, or controllers.
- Group constants logically by domain (`app.ts`, `currency.ts`, `pipeline.ts`, `logger.ts`) and export via `constants/index.ts`.
- Enforce >= 90% unit test coverage on all constants files.

# Separate Data Types & Interfaces Rules
- Keep all data types, interfaces, enums, and type aliases in separate files under `types/`.
- Never declare `interface` or `type` definitions directly in component (`.tsx`), controller, or service files.
- Move all component & modal prop interfaces to dedicated type files (e.g. `types/components.ts`) and import them.
- Re-export all types via `types/index.ts`.

# Input Schema Validation Rules
- Enforce strict schema validation for all user and system inputs (forms, modals, API routes, controller bodies, query parameters, headers).
- Define all validation schemas in dedicated constants modules under `constants/` (`frontend/src/constants/validation.ts` and `backend/src/constants/validation.ts`).
- Never declare validation schemas inline.
- Keep derived types in `types/validation.ts` re-exported via `types/index.ts`.
- Reject invalid inputs immediately with HTTP 400 Bad Request.
- Maintain >= 90% unit test coverage for all validation schemas and utilities.


